  "info": {
    "indicator_code": "30771924",
    "describe": "The final-demand subcomponent of the Producer Price Index (PPI) measures changes in the prices domestic producers receive when selling goods and services for final use, covering the complete price pressure across demand-side categories including personal consumption, capital investment, and government procurement. Unlike the intermediate-goods PPI, the final-demand PPI more directly reflects the degree of transmission of production-side inflation to end markets. These data are not seasonally adjusted and retain the raw price swings, so care is needed in interpreting them during periods with strong seasonal factors. The year-on-year basis compares the current month with the same month of the previous year, effectively removing short-term distortions and presenting a more trend-oriented direction of price change. The indicator is an important forward-looking signal for market judgments of the inflation trend: a rising PPI often foreshadows rising future CPI pressure and directly affects the direction of Federal Reserve policy and corporate-earnings expectations, drawing wide attention from macro investors.",
    "country": "US",
    "name": "United States, Producer Prices, Final demand, Chg Y/Y",
